{"author":"Rebar Bot Nava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\nAfter an MCP blue-green cutover, `gate_status` on the new container reports a job that is still running on the draining container as `failed / daemon-stopped`, and writes that into the shared index. Callers then start a duplicate, billable gate run.\n\n### Observation (read-only, 2026-11-18 PST)\nTwo occurrences on 2026-11-16, both on `sarcastic-specified-kodiakbear` (times UTC):\n- `verify_completion_start` returned job `1794871413492186335-b6a138…` at 23:23:33. Autodeploy cut `/mcp` over to a new container (8093) at 23:23:27 and retired `rebar-mcp-b4e0d01b6491-8092` with `docker stop --time 1260` (graceful drain). At 23:24:36, `gate_status` returned `status: failed, verdict: daemon-stopped`. The index file then ended `passed / PASS`, written 23:25:36 by the draining container's daemon.\n- `review_plan_start` returned job `1794872550672894186-492f05…` at 23:42:30. Cutover to 8092 came at 23:44:25. At 23:49:37, `gate_status` returned `failed / daemon-stopped`. The index ended `passed / PASS` at 23:51:57.\nThe caller acted on the false failure and started a duplicate, billable run each time (23:24:45 and 23:49:58).\n\n### Mechanism (from code)\n`_running_record_is_stale` (`src/rebar/llm/gate_runs.py:293`) treats a `running` index record as crashed whenever `_mcp_inflight.is_job_active(job_id)` (`src/rebar/_mcp_inflight.py:135`) is false. That registry lives in process memory. Both containers share the `.rebar/gate_runs` index on the `gerrit_mcp_code` volume, so the new container has no registry entry for the old container's job. `_materialize_stale_running` (`gate_runs.py:308`) then writes `failed` into the shared index, and the old daemon overwrites it later. Autodeploy keeps the old container draining for up to 1,260 s, so every gate that spans a cutover takes this path.\n\n## Approach\nGive each job an owner lock that the kernel releases when the owning process dies, and have the poller ask the lock instead of only its own memory.\n1. Owner side (`src/rebar/_mcp_llm.py`, `_start_gate_job` at :257 and `_spawn_gate_daemon` at :218): the leader opens `.rebar/gate_runs/<job_id>.lock` and takes `rebar._store.lock_kernel.take_exclusive` (`lock_kernel.py:120`; `fcntl.flock` on POSIX, `msvcrt.locking` on Windows) before it writes the `running` record. The daemon's `finally` writes the terminal record first, then closes the fd and best-effort deletes the lock file. If the lock cannot be taken, the job still runs and pollers fall back to today's rule.\n2. Poller side (`gate_runs.py`): a new helper `_owner_lock_held(job_id, repo_root)` opens the existing lock file without creating it and tries `take_exclusive`; `is_contention` (`lock_kernel.py:185`) means a live owner holds it. `_running_record_is_stale` returns False when the owner lock is held. A missing lock file, or a lock that can be taken, keeps today's behaviour (stale after the grace window). `_materialize_stale_running` already re-reads the record, so an owner that finishes between the probe and the write still wins.\nWhy a kernel lock: the two containers have separate PID namespaces, so a stamped PID cannot be checked across them, but `flock` is held on the shared inode under one kernel and is dropped when the holder dies. The store write lock relies on the same property (`lock_kernel.py` docstring). A heartbeat would need a time threshold; the lock needs none.\nThe owner-lock suffix is the literal `.lock` (`_OWNER_LOCK_SUFFIX`, `src/rebar/llm/gate_runs.py:106`). Its census key `lock::.lock` is already in `.github/mechanism-baseline.json:606`, so `python scripts/check_mechanism_delta.py --check` stays green without a marker. No `# mechanism-ok` marker is added: one naming this ticket would make `close_precheck._refuse_open_sunset_marker` (`src/rebar/_commands/close_precheck.py:554-562`) refuse this ticket's close. No config key, env var or flag.\nPortability: `lock_kernel` already provides both legs, and paths use `pathlib`; the test helper process is started with `sys.executable` and pipes, no signals or `fork`.\n\n## Scope\n- `src/rebar/llm/gate_runs.py`: owner-lock helpers, `_running_record_is_stale`.\n- `src/rebar/_mcp_llm.py`: take and release the owner lock.\n- `tests/unit/test_mcp_gate_async.py`: regression tests.\n\n## Testing\n- Live owner in another process: a helper Python process (started with `sys.executable`) takes the owner lock for job `J`; the test writes a `running` record for `J` older than the grace window with an empty in-process registry; `gate_run_status(J)` returns `running` and the index file is unchanged.\n- Owner died: the same helper exits (stdin closed), then `gate_run_status(J)` returns `failed / daemon-stopped` (existing tests for records with no lock file stay green).\n- End to end: a `review_plan_start` with a blocking stub leaves `<job_id>.lock` held while running and released after the terminal record is written.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [x] A regression test simulates a job whose `running` index record was written by another process that is still alive, and asserts `gate_status` does not report `failed` or overwrite the index while that owner is live; `pytest tests/unit/test_mcp_gate_async.py` passes.\n- [x] A job whose owning process really died is still materialized as failed (existing behaviour kept, test kept green).\n- [x] `python scripts/check_mechanism_delta.py --check` exits 0 and `make verify` passes.\n- [x] After merge, the `sweep (windows)` job of `test.yml` on main passes."}},"env_id":"492a5c71-6cda-48a6-8134-02ff6a672739","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1795125129047417001,"uuid":"dbeed98b-035a-40fc-8947-6aeb40e76f0a"}